    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A tank water heater typically lasts 8 to 12 years if it burns gas and 10 to 15 years if it is electric, and the Department of Energy puts conventional storage heaters as a group at 10 to 15 years. Where yours lands depends less on the brand than on three things: how fast the anode rod is used up, how much sediment your water leaves in the tank, and whether anyone ever drains it. Here is how to work out how old yours is, which warning signs mean it is nearly done, and when a repair is still worth paying for.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: 8 to 12 Years for Gas, 10 to 15 for Electric
          </h2>

          <p>
            Those ranges are the trade's working numbers, and they line up with the Department of Energy's Energy Saver guidance, which gives conventional storage water heaters a life expectancy of 10 to 15 years. They describe a glass-lined steel tank on ordinary water with ordinary maintenance, which in most houses means no maintenance at all. A heater that gets looked after can beat them. One on harsh water that nobody touches can fall well short.
          </p>

          <p>
            What ends a tank heater's life is almost always the same thing: the steel tank corrodes through and starts to leak. Thermostats, heating elements, gas valves and thermocouples fail too, but those are replaceable parts, and a plumber can swap most of them in an hour. A leaking tank cannot be repaired. So the question of how long a water heater lasts is really the question of how long the tank can hold off rust.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8-12 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical gas tank lifespan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10-15 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical electric tank lifespan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">120°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DOE-suggested thermostat setting for most homes</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">6-12 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common residential tank warranty terms</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The warranty is a useful clue. Residential tanks are commonly sold with 6, 8, 10 or 12-year tank warranties, and the longer-warranty version of a model often differs mainly in having a larger or second anode rod and sometimes thicker insulation. In other words, the extra money buys corrosion protection, which is exactly what decides tank life. A 6-year heater is not designed to die at year six, but it has less margin to spend.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat the range as a planning window rather than a prediction. Once a heater passes about 10 years it is in the zone where a leak could come any month, and failures rarely arrive at a convenient time. If yours is that old, decide now what you would replace it with and how it would be vented, so a leak becomes a phone call rather than a scramble. Our guide to the <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best gas water heaters</a> compares the tanks we would shortlist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-vs-electric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Do Gas Water Heaters Die Sooner Than Electric?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A gas tank has a burner underneath it and a flue running straight up through the middle of the water. The bottom of the tank sees direct flame, so the steel there runs hotter and goes through bigger temperature swings than anything inside an electric tank. Sediment that settles on that hot bottom makes it worse. The flue tube is a second weak point, attacked by water on one side and by moist combustion gases on the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            An electric tank heats with elements immersed in the water, so there is no flame against the steel and no flue through the centre. The shell lives an easier life. Its weak points are the elements and thermostats, which burn out, scale up and occasionally fail, but they are cheap parts that a handy owner can replace. That is the main reason electric tanks cluster toward the top of the range and gas tanks toward the bottom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat pump water heaters are electric tanks with a compressor and fan on top. The tank underneath is the same kind of glass-lined steel, protected the same way, so its life follows the same anode and sediment rules, while the refrigeration parts add things that can fail. Expect roughly the same 10 to 15 years as other tanks, and note that many models carry a 10-year warranty. Our <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best heat pump water heaters</a> guide covers which ones we trust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tankless heaters are a different machine with a different answer. With no tank to rust, a well-maintained unit commonly reaches 20 years, and its lifespan is governed by scale in the heat exchanger rather than corrosion. We cover that in detail in <a href="/how-long-do-tankless-water-heaters-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long tankless water heaters last</a>. If you are replacing a tank and staying in the house a long time, the extra years are part of what tankless buys, although they rarely pay back the higher installed cost on their own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="anode-rod" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is the Anode Rod, and Why Does It Decide the Lifespan?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every glass-lined tank has at least one anode rod: a steel core wire coated in magnesium or an aluminum alloy that screws into the top of the tank, either through its own hex port or combined with the hot outlet nipple. The glass lining always has tiny flaws, and water reaching bare steel through them would start rusting it. The anode is more chemically reactive than steel, so corrosion attacks the rod instead. It is a sacrificial part, meant to be eaten.
          </p>

          <p>
            While there is anode left, the tank is protected. Once the rod is consumed down to its core wire, corrosion moves to the tank, and the countdown to a leak starts in earnest. That makes the anode the single best predictor of tank life, and replacing it, typically a part costing somewhere around $20 to $60, the most effective thing an owner can do to push a heater past its expected years. A tank that has already started leaking is past saving.
          </p>

          <p>
            How fast the rod goes depends on your water. Softened water is the classic accelerator, because it is more conductive and consumes anodes noticeably faster. Very hard water, high-chloride water and hotter thermostat settings also speed things up. Rather than a fixed replacement date, a common recommendation is to pull and inspect the rod every two to three years, or every year in a softened home, starting around year three on a new heater.
          </p>

          <p>
            The inspection is a modest job. Turn off the power or set the gas valve to pilot or vacation, shut the cold supply, open a hot tap to relieve pressure, and drain a few gallons. The hex head on top is usually 1-1/16 inch and very tight, so a breaker bar helps. Replace the rod if several inches of core wire are exposed or it is worn to roughly half its original thickness. Where ceiling height is short, a segmented link-style anode bends in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two cautions. If the hot water smells of sulfur, swapping a magnesium anode for an aluminum-zinc one often cures it, which we explain in <a href="/why-does-my-water-smell-like-rotten-eggs/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">why your water smells like rotten eggs</a>. And never pull the anode out and leave it out, whether to kill a smell or to save effort. The tank will then corrode quickly, and manufacturers generally treat a removed anode as voiding the tank warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sediment" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Do Sediment and Hard Water Shorten It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hard water carries dissolved calcium and magnesium, and heating water drives those minerals out of solution. In a tank they form scale on electric elements and settle as a layer of sediment on the bottom, and well water can add sand and grit on top of that. Left alone for years, that layer can grow to several inches, taking up hot water capacity and making the heater work harder for every gallon.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a gas heater, sediment is a direct threat to the tank. It sits between the burner and the water, so the steel bottom runs hotter, the burner runs longer, and the lining is stressed. The rumbling, popping or crackling a gas tank makes when the burner fires is usually water trapped under sediment flashing to steam. On an electric heater, the lower element can end up buried in sediment, overheat and burn out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The remedy is flushing, which manufacturers generally recommend at least once a year. Shut off the power or gas, attach a hose to the drain valve, open a hot tap to break the vacuum, and drain until the water runs clear, briefly opening the cold supply to stir up the bottom. One caveat for tanks that have never been flushed: the plastic drain valve may clog or refuse to reseal, so have a brass replacement valve or a threaded cap ready before you start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature and pressure matter too. The DOE suggests 120°F for most homes, and every degree above that speeds scale and corrosion while wasting energy. In a closed plumbing system, one with a check valve or pressure-reducing valve on the main, heated water has nowhere to expand, so pressure spikes on every heating cycle and strains the tank. A thermal expansion tank absorbs that, and many local codes now require one on closed systems.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your water is very hard, a softener protects the heater from scale, at the cost of a faster-wearing anode. That trade is usually worth making as long as you check the rod more often. Our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best water softener systems</a> guide covers sizing, and <a href="/how-long-do-water-softeners-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long water softeners last</a> explains what you are signing up for in upkeep.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-old" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Old Is My Water Heater? Reading the Serial Number
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the rating label on the side of the tank. It lists the model and serial number, and many newer labels also print the manufacture date outright, which saves any decoding. If not, the date is almost always hidden in the serial number, and each manufacturer encodes it differently. Once you know the brand, a minute with the right format usually tells you the month and year.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Brand</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where the date usually is</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Example</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rheem, Ruud</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">First four digits after any leading letters: month, then year</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0815 = August 2015</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A.O. Smith, State (recent units)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">First four digits: year, then week</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1532 = week 32 of 2015</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bradford White</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">First two letters: year, then month, on a repeating letter cycle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check the brand's decoder chart</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Any brand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A printed manufacture date on the label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The most reliable source</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            These are the commonly used formats, but they have changed over the decades and older units may follow a different pattern. Bradford White's letter code repeats on a 20-year cycle, so you need to judge which cycle fits the look of the heater. Every major manufacturer publishes a serial-number decoder or will confirm the date by phone if you give them the full serial, and that is worth doing if the answer decides whether you replace.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are other clues if the label is unreadable. Plumbers often write the install date on the tank in marker. A permit record, a home inspection report from when you bought the house, or a receipt in the house file can all settle it. Remember that the manufacture date and the install date are not the same: a heater can sit in a warehouse for months, so the installed age is usually a little younger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then put the age next to your conditions. An electric tank at year nine on soft city water, flushed now and then, is in no particular danger. A gas tank at year eleven on hard well water that has never been drained is living on borrowed time, even if it is working perfectly today. The same age means different things in different houses, which is why the warning signs below matter as much as the birthday.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warning-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Are the Warning Signs a Water Heater Is Failing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water at the base is the one everyone worries about, but not every puddle means a dead tank. Find where it comes from first. A dripping temperature and pressure relief valve or its discharge pipe, a weeping drain valve, or a leaking fitting on top are all fixable. Brief condensation on a gas tank heating very cold incoming water is normal. Water seeping from under the jacket while every fitting is dry is the tank itself, and that means replacement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rusty hot water is the next sign to take seriously. If only the hot side runs brown or orange, either the anode is spent or the tank lining is failing, although old galvanized pipes can cause the same thing. Drain a few gallons from the tank's own valve: rusty water straight out of the tank points at the heater. If the cold side is discoloured too, the problem is in the supply or the pipes, not the heater.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some symptoms sound alarming but are usually repairable. Rumbling and popping mean sediment, which a flush may relieve if it is not too late. Running out of hot water quickly can be a failed upper or lower element, a broken dip tube, a faulty thermostat or a tank half full of sediment. A gas pilot that will not stay lit usually points at the thermocouple or flame sensor. None of those means the tank is finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some signs need action straight away. A relief valve that discharges repeatedly is telling you the tank is overheating or over-pressurised, and it should never be capped or plugged. Soot or scorch marks around the burner access, a lazy yellow flame or any smell of gas mean shut off the gas supply and call a professional, or your gas utility if you smell gas in the air. These are safety problems, not lifespan problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            Age itself is the quiet sign. A heater past 10 years that starts showing any of the above, particularly rusty hot water or moisture around the base, is telling you the end is close. Because a tank failure can dump its full contents onto the floor, a simple water alarm next to the heater is cheap insurance; our guide to <a href="/basement-water-alarm/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement water alarms</a> covers the inexpensive ones that work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Should You Repair It or Replace It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first rule is absolute: a leaking tank gets replaced. There is no patch, sealant or weld that makes a corroded tank safe to keep. Almost everything else on a tank heater is a part, and parts can be swapped. The decision is really about whether a given repair makes sense on a heater of a given age, and the table below gives the usual answer.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Problem</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Usual answer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tank leaking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replace</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A corroded tank cannot be repaired</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Element or thermostat (electric)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Repair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cheap parts, often a DIY job</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thermocouple or flame sensor (gas)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Repair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inexpensive, quick for a pro</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas control valve, older heater</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually replace</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Costly part on a tank near end of life</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Relief or drain valve</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Repair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard replaceable parts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spent anode, no leak yet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Repair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adds years if the tank is sound</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A sensible rule of thumb: inside the first half of a heater's expected life, almost any repair short of a tank leak is worth doing. Past roughly 10 years, if a repair costs more than about half of a new installed heater, put the money toward the replacement instead. A gas control valve on an 11-year-old tank is the classic example: an expensive part protecting a tank that may leak next year anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacement cost varies more than people expect. A standard 40 or 50-gallon tank installed commonly lands somewhere between roughly $1,200 and $3,000, and more for power-vent, high-efficiency or heat pump models. Code upgrades often add to the bill: an expansion tank, a drain pan, seismic strapping in some regions, or venting changes. Get the quote while the old heater still works and you can compare options calmly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then make the new one last. Set it to 120°F, flush it every year, pull the anode at around year three and on a schedule after that, more often if you soften, and fit an expansion tank if your system is closed. None of that takes more than an afternoon a year, and it is the difference between a heater that sees 8 years and one that sees 14. Our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">gas water heater rankings</a> note which tanks make anode access easiest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting for the leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most people replace a water heater the day it floods the utility room, which means paying for an emergency call, accepting whatever unit the plumber has on the truck, and cleaning up water damage. Once a tank passes 10 years, choose the replacement and get a quote in advance, so the failure costs you a phone call instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing the anode to stop a smell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pulling the anode rod does stop the sulfur smell in hot water, which is why the advice keeps circulating. It also leaves the steel tank with no corrosion protection, so it rusts through years early, and most manufacturers treat it as voiding the warranty. Fit an aluminum-zinc anode or a powered anode instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flushing a neglected tank without a plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A first flush on a tank that has gone eight or more years without one can clog the drain valve with sediment or leave a brittle plastic valve that will not close. Have a brass replacement valve or cap and a bucket ready, and do not force a seized valve with the tank full.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Turning up the thermostat to fix a shortage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running short of hot water is a symptom, usually of sediment, a failed element or a broken dip tube. Raising the setting to 140°F or more hides it for a while, speeds scale and corrosion, wastes energy and increases scald risk. Find the cause first, and keep the setting near 120°F.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every puddle is a dead tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water near a heater can come from the relief valve, the drain valve, a top fitting or condensation, and all of those are fixable for far less than a new heater. Dry everything, put down paper towels, and watch where the water reappears before you commit thousands of dollars to a replacement.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do electric water heaters last longer than gas ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Electric tanks typically last 10 to 15 years against 8 to 12 for gas, because there is no burner heating the tank bottom and no flue running through the water. Their common failures, elements and thermostats, are cheap and replaceable. Both types still end the same way, when the steel tank corrodes through, so anode care matters on either.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a tank water heater last 20 years?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some do, typically electric tanks on soft or moderate water whose anode rods were replaced and whose tanks were flushed regularly. It is not the norm, and a 20-year-old tank is living on luck rather than design. If yours is that old and still working, keep a water alarm beside it and have a replacement plan ready rather than assuming it will continue.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a water heater anode rod be replaced?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no fixed interval, because water chemistry decides how fast it is consumed. A common approach is to inspect it every two to three years starting around year three, or yearly in a home with a water softener, and replace it when several inches of core wire are exposed or the rod is worn to about half its original thickness.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a water softener shorten water heater life?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It cuts both ways. Softened water stops the scale and sediment that damage tanks and burn out elements, which helps. But it is more conductive, so the anode rod is consumed faster, and once the anode is gone the tank corrodes. With a softener, check the anode more often, roughly every year, and the net effect on tank life is usually positive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I flush a water heater that has never been flushed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes, but with care. On a tank several years old, a flush removes sediment that is stressing the bottom and stealing capacity. On a tank ten or more years old with no history, the drain valve may clog or fail to reseal, and disturbing heavy sediment can occasionally expose a weak spot. Have a replacement valve ready, and weigh the heater's age.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to replace a water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standard 40 or 50-gallon tank, installed, commonly falls between roughly $1,200 and $3,000, depending on fuel type, the model, local labour rates and any code upgrades such as an expansion tank, drain pan or venting changes. Power-vent, high-efficiency and heat pump models cost more. Planned replacements are usually cheaper than emergency ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I replace a 15-year-old water heater that still works?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is reasonable to replace it proactively, especially if it is a gas tank, sits where a leak would damage finished space, or shows rust in the hot water. It is already past the typical range. If it sits on a concrete floor near a drain and shows no signs, running it longer with a water alarm next to it is a defensible choice.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

